    {
      "block_id": "2605.00155_p11_b23",
      "text": "Theorem 3.5 is a genuine water-filling rule, but with an RLHF-specific twist. All non-\nmaximal responses receive precisely the excess of their proxy reward above the optimal\n",
      "bbox": [
        71.58,
        372.07,
        525.24,
        402.06
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        286,
        1488,
        2100,
        1608
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 12.07,
      "font_name": "LMRoman12-Regular",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 173,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00155_p11_b24",
      "text": "threshold t\u22c6, while the remaining probability mass is assigned to the best response. When\n\u03b4 is small, the threshold is high and the policy stays concentrated. As \u03b4 grows, new\nresponses enter the hedge. Once \u03b4 is large enough that t\u22c6= t0 < \u02c6rn, every response\nreceives positive mass and the policy approaches the uniform distribution. Section E\ncontains the proof. For the main text, the important point is qualitative: the ambiguity\n",
      "bbox": [
        72.0,
        406.81,
        523.79,
        491.69
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        288,
        1627,
        2095,
        1966
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 11.84,
      "font_name": "LMRoman12-Regular",
      "is_math": true,
      "char_count": 432,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00155_p11_b25",
      "text": "budget lowers a threshold through the ordered proxy rewards, and probability is assigned\nonly to responses whose uncovered rewards remain above that threshold.\n",
      "bbox": [
        72.0,
        497.74,
        523.35,
        527.54
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        288,
        1990,
        2093,
        2110
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 11.85,
      "font_name": "LMRoman12-Regular",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 159,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00155_p11_b26",
      "text": "3.3\nInterpreting Water-Filling Policy\n",
      "bbox": [
        72.0,
        552.68,
        342.34,
        567.02
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        288,
        2210,
        1369,
        2268
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 14.35,
      "font_name": "LMRoman12-Bold",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 37,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00155_p11_b27",
      "text": "The water-filling theorem is useful not only because it is explicit, but also because it\n",
      "bbox": [
        71.58,
        580.12,
        523.61,
        592.2
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        286,
        2320,
        2094,
        2368
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 12.07,
      "font_name": "LMRoman12-Regular",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 88,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00155_p11_b28",
      "text": "explains how the robustness budget changes the policy qualitatively. The policy does not\nflatten all responses equally. It first hedges against nearby competitors, then gradually\n",
      "bbox": [
        72.0,
        598.21,
        523.77,
        628.05
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        288,
        2392,
        2095,
        2512
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 11.87,
      "font_name": "LMRoman12-Regular",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 178,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00155_p11_b29",
      "text": "widens support as the ambiguity budget grows, and it always keeps any residual probability\nmass on the top-ranked response.\n",
      "bbox": [
        71.58,
        634.09,
        523.77,
        663.88
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        286,
        2536,
        2095,
        2655
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 11.84,
      "font_name": "LMRoman12-Regular",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 123,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00155_p11_b30",
      "text": "Figure 2 makes the mechanics of the optimizer visible. Small ambiguity budgets trust the\nproxy and therefore keep most probability on the best response, with only the nearest\ncompetitor receiving hedge mass.\nAs \u03b4 increases, the threshold t\u22c6falls and further\nresponses enter the active support one by one. What matters is not their absolute rank\nalone, but whether their uncovered rewards still sit above the current water level.\n",
      "bbox": [
        72.0,
        669.95,
        523.64,
        753.52
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        288,
        2679,
        2094,
        3014
      ],
